= Inductive bias =

== Overview ==
An '''inductive bias''' is the set of assumptions, preferences, constraints, or prior structure that enables a learning system to choose among hypotheses compatible with observed data and make predictions beyond those observations.

Generalization requires some form of inductive bias because finite data usually support many possible hypotheses.

== Forms ==
Inductive bias can enter through:

* hypothesis-class restrictions;
* architecture;
* priors;
* regularization;
* invariances;
* locality or smoothness assumptions;
* data augmentation;
* optimization dynamics;
* simplicity preferences.

Bias can be explicit or implicit.

== Bias is not necessarily error ==
“Bias” here does not mean cognitive distortion or unfairness.

A useful inductive bias can greatly improve generalization when it matches the task structure.

A mismatched bias can hurt performance.

== No-free-lunch perspective ==
Without assumptions relating observed and future data, no learner can prefer one generalization over every alternative.

Inductive bias makes learning possible by constraining that space.

== Related concepts ==
[[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Machine learning]], [[Hypothesis]], [[Inference]], [[Cognitive bias]], and [[Model selection bias]] distinguish inductive assumptions from other senses of bias.

